eople are protesting about race  relations in this country  you were writing about this very thing  in 1969  so i'm thinking what message does it  send to you at this moment  the timing of it all well i mean uh it  just shows that we  we still have um a little bit of you  know distance to cover but  that some of these problems are not new  and and uh it's really depends on how  you look at things  you know in terms of addressing them  they're complicated problems but  um they require uh you know complicated  and  and humane answers  you've got an interesting there are many  interesting dynamics in relationships in  the book but one of them is about a  pastor's wife  who has a friendship with a white police  officer sister g and she says this  you all don't watch out for us y'all  watch over us  how did you how did you approach your  portrayal of police officers during this  time  and listen this book was written way  before what we're going through now but  as you said  this is not a new new issue  well i mean you know like like any  african  like many black person i've had my  issues with the police but on the other  hand you know i have  family members who are police officers  and and i know that most cops don't go  to bed  you know they're not they're not  thinking of racism when they go to bed  they  probably worry about their wife sleeping  with the gardener or something so i  didn't really  um i wanted to humanize a part of the  world that most of us never see  and that included the people in the  projects as well as the officers  who uh who guard them that particular  character you're talking about  happens to fall in love with with sister  i don't want to give the book away but  sister g and that officer  um they end up they end up falling in  love which is you know  it happens in this world and um my job  as a writer is to show that kind of  thing to people because that's  that's really that's what really lasts  the it's the love that counts it's the  love 